+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -euo pipefail
+cat <<'EOF'
+RT Kernel & system tuning helper (operator-run)
+
+This script prints recommended steps to prepare a Linux node for real-time servo loops.
+It does not change your system automatically — follow and adapt to your environment.
+
+High-level steps:
+ 1. Install an RT kernel (e.g., ubuntu-rt, PREEMPT_RT) or use a real-time capable distro.
+ 2. Configure GRUB to boot the RT kernel.
+ 3. Configure CPU isolation via kernel boot param: isolcpus=<cpu-list> and irqaffinity.
+ 4. Disable irqbalance or pin irq affinities to isolated CPUs.
+ 5. Mlockall permissions (LimitMEMLOCK) and set CAP_SYS_NICE for controller binary.
+ 6. Tune NIC and enable busy-poll where appropriate.
+
+Examples (operator must run with root privileges and validate for their distro):
+  - Install RT kernel (Ubuntu example):
+      sudo apt-get install linux-image-rt
+  - Add kernel boot param (example):
+      GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="quiet splash isolcpus=2,3 nohz_full=2,3 rcu_nocbs=2,3"
+      sudo update-grub
+  - Reboot into RT kernel.
+
+Network / NIC busy-poll tuning:
+  - Enable busy poll at runtime:
+      sudo sysctl -w net.core.busy_poll=50
+      sudo sysctl -w net.core.busy_read=50
+  - Increase NIC ring sizes:
+      sudo ethtool -G eth0 rx 4096 tx 4096
+  - Reduce coalescing / rx-usecs:
+      sudo ethtool -C eth0 rx-usecs 10 rx-frames 64
+
+CPU isolation sample:
+  - Boot with isolcpus and nohz_full as above.
+  - Put real-time controller on an isolated CPU (see systemd unit in ops/robotics/rt_controller/rt_controller.service)
+
+Notes:
+ - Kernel and NIC tuning are hardware & distro dependent — test in staging.
+ - Use vendor docs for NIC drivers and interrupts for best results.
+EOF
+
